title: against discipline
pid: e4b11be8-1f32-4a50-8768-4abd39a297ed
body: |2-
  I think discipline is overrated. It is emotional violence. It's only required when you're fighting yourself, and you're only fighting yourself when part of you doesn't think the way you've chosen is the right one.

  For myself, discpline has been a stand-in for deeper feelings:
  - Insecurity. This led, for instance, to a lot of "discilpined" working out, which I don't regret, but I think it helps to be clear-eyed about where it came from.
  - Hopelessness. That's how I felt in the Ph.D. program. In this case it did not actually result in disciplined behavior, just the wish that I had discipline -- masking a bigger wish, which I never voiced until years later, that I didn't consider the work pointless.
  - Love. I played guitar or piano for four hours a day for years. People told me this was discipline and I believed them, but it wasn't. If anything it was indiscipline -- I ran away from lots of problems by making music.
  - Shame/love. This is why I'm a good guest at my mom's place -- finally, in my forties. I just have to pay attention to the effect I'm having on her, and it leads to behavior (cooking, cleaning) that looks like discipline.
